  "theorem": "Monster Moonshine W(3,3) Theorem.  (1) The Monster group |M| has exactly 15 = g prime divisors, where 15 = M_4 (Mersenne) = T_5 (triangular) = C(6,2) = SM gauge generators.  (2) The first six prime exponents of |M| are exactly the W(3,3) primitives: (2, 46), (3, 20), (5, 9), (7, 6), (11, 2), (13, 3) with exponents v + q!, 2 Theta, q^2, q!, lambda, q.  (3) The j-invariant constants decompose: 744 = q * dim(E_8) = 31 * f = q * (E + lambda^q), and 196884 = E q^2 Phi_6 Phi_3 + mu q^4 = 196560 (Leech kissing) + 324 (mu q^4).  (4) Ramanujan tau satisfies tau(2) = -f and tau(3) = C(Theta, q + lambda) = 252.  All five 'central moonshine integers' {12, 24, 27, 54, 248} have direct W(3,3) names {k, f, q^q, 2q^q, E + 2^q}.",
  "one_line": "Monster has 15 = g prime divisors; first 6 exponents are W(3,3) primitives; j(tau) constants 744 = q*dim(E_8) and 196884 = Leech kissing + mu*q^4.",
  "honesty_boundary": "All identities are exact integer arithmetic, drawn from the W(3,3) paper's Supplement I (Monster Moonshine Bridge) and Part XII (Moonshine Chain).  This part consolidates and verifies them numerically.  It does NOT prove a functorial connection between W(3,3) and Monstrous Moonshine; it documents the exact NUMERICAL coincidences of integers in both arithmetic skeletons (the paper's 'same arithmetic in two different cathedrals' phenomenon)."
